Usage: 
 $ xpuprof PROG [ARGS]
 Klprof will start before executing of PROG and stop after PROG exit

General:
 -b BUFSIZE, --buffer-size=BUFSIZE
             specify ring-buffer size for record xpu trace data in MB (default 0)
 -x DEVIDS, --xpu=DEVIDS
             enable trace on xpu with DEVIDS
             e.g. --xpu=0,1,2 or --xpu=all
 -d, --deamonize
             xpuprof will be triggered by xpu_profiler_start/stop
 -t TIMES, --time=TIMES
             xpuprof will run TIMES seconds and then exit
 -V
             version check
 -h, --help
             print help information
Note: 
  Root privleges and kunlun-driver version higher than 4.0.28 needed for using xpuprof
  User can only select one running mode from PROG [ARGS] or --time or --deamonize

Metrics:
 -A, --profile-api-trace
             xpuprof can collect information from CPU-side XRE API, root or sudo access required
 -s, --sse-trace
             kernel execution flow will be recorded.
 -S, --settings
             xpuprof read signal settings from this json file
             a default settings (enable --profile-api-trace and --sse-trace) will be used without of this option.

Timer:
 -r INTERVAL, --resolution-interval=INTERVAL
             tracer timer interval in ns (default 0)

Output format:
 -e PROFILE, --export-profile=PROFILE
             save profiling result to PROFILE
             If PROFILE is a directory, content will be saved inside it
             If PROFILE is a file and exists already, content will be overwritten
             User can display this json file in chrome://tracing/.
